  "image_name": "1e3c908858cccc0da0c0e67c81e21f30.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how epigenetic variation in plants can be generated, maintained, and selected through grafting and tissue culture. A central grafted plant is shown with a rootstock and scion separated by a graft line. One branch displays a differently colored shoot, indicating a spontaneous epiallele that is mitotically heritable, and this altered state can be propagated as an epigenetic “sport” into a whole plant with the same visible trait.\n\nA second part illustrates a clonal selection workflow. Tissue is taken from a parent plant, placed on nutrient agar, and regenerated into many plantlets. These clones are then profiled and screened, allowing selection of individuals with useful epiallelic variants. The emphasis is on producing many genetically similar plants and identifying stable epigenetic differences among them.\n\nThe final part highlights graft-mediated transfer of epigenetic information. Signals appear to move from the rootstock into the scion across the graft, with small RNAs from the rootstock triggering RNA-directed DNA methylation in the scion. Overall, the figure presents plant grafting and regeneration as practical routes for creating and transmitting heritable epigenetic states without changing DNA sequence.",
